# Copy to `.env` (which is git-ignored) before running `astro dev start`:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# The Astro CLI injects every variable in `.env` into the Airflow containers.
# Defining connections as AIRFLOW_CONN_* environment variables means the
# pipeline works on a clean `astro dev start` with no clicking in the UI, and no
# credentials committed to Git.
#
# Naming rule: AIRFLOW_CONN_<CONN_ID_UPPERCASED>. The DAG asks for the
# connection IDs `open_meteo_api` and `postgres_default`, so the variables are
# AIRFLOW_CONN_OPEN_METEO_API and AIRFLOW_CONN_POSTGRES_DEFAULT.
#
# NOTE: environment-variable connections do not appear in the Airflow UI's
# Admin -> Connections list. That is expected; they still resolve at runtime.
# Precedence is: environment variable > secrets backend > metadata database.

# --- Source: Open-Meteo public REST API (no API key required) ---------------
# `host` must include the scheme; HttpHook joins it with the endpoint the DAG
# builds, producing https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?...
AIRFLOW_CONN_OPEN_METEO_API='{"conn_type": "http", "host": "https://api.open-meteo.com"}'

# --- Target: the weather warehouse defined in docker-compose.override.yml ---
# `host` is the Compose SERVICE NAME (weather_db), resolved over the shared
# `airflow` Docker network — not localhost, and not host.docker.internal.
# `port` is the CONTAINER port 5432, not the published host port 5434.
#
# WARNING: `postgres_default` is a connection ID that Airflow pre-creates in its
# metadata DB pointing at Airflow's OWN database. This variable overrides it.
# Without the override the DAG would silently write `weather_data` into the
# Airflow metadata database.
AIRFLOW_CONN_POSTGRES_DEFAULT='{"conn_type": "postgres", "login": "postgres", "password": "postgres", "host": "weather_db", "port": 5432, "schema": "weather"}'
